1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,026
NATALIE: Dave, I'm worried about our case study,

2
00:00:04,026 --> 00:00:07,560
I've done a bit of reading

3
00:00:07,720 --> 00:00:11,272
but I'm not sure what's involved in actually writing a case study -

4
00:00:11,272 --> 00:00:15,700
I missed the lecture where Dr Baker talked us through it.

5
00:00:15,700 --> 00:00:18,540
DAVE: OK, well it's quite straightforward

6
00:00:19,040 --> 00:00:24,004
We've got our focus - that's tourism at the Horton Castle site.

7
00:00:24,004 --> 00:00:28,400
And you said you'd done some reading about it.

8
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:33,770
NATALIE: Yes, I found some articles and made notes of the main points.

9
00:00:33,770 --> 00:00:38,660
DAVE: Did you remember to keep a record of where you got the information from?

10
00:00:38,660 --> 00:00:42,870
NATALIE: Sure. I know what a pain it is when you forget that.

11
00:00:42,870 --> 00:00:46,740
DAVE: OK, so we can compare what we've read.

12
00:00:46,740 --> 00:00:52,760
Then we have to decide on a particular problem or need at our site.

13
00:00:52,760 --> 00:00:58,350
And then think about who we're going to interview to get more information.

14
00:00:58,350 --> 00:01:04,020
NATALIE: OK. So who'd that be? The people who work there

15
00:01:04,380 --> 00:01:07,610
And presumably some of the tourists too?

16
00:01:07,610 --> 00:01:15,181
DAVE: Yes, both those groups. So we'll have to go to the site to do that, I suppose.

17
00:01:15,181 --> 00:01:20,650
But we might also do some of our interviewing away from the site -

18
00:01:20,650 --> 00:01:22,660
we could even contact some people here in the city

19
00:01:23,220 --> 00:01:27,380
like administrators involved in overseeing tourism.

20
00:01:27,380 --> 00:01:32,200
NATALIE: OK. So we'll need to think about our interview questions

21
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:35,204
and fix times and places for the meetings.

22
00:01:35,204 --> 00:01:38,910
It's all going to take a lot of time.

23
00:01:38,910 --> 00:01:41,301
DAVE: Mmm. And if we can,

24
00:01:41,301 --> 00:01:43,260
we should ask our interviewees

25
00:01:43,260 --> 00:01:49,670
if they can bring along some numerical data that we can add to support our findings.

26
00:01:49,670 --> 00:01:51,890
NATALIE: And photographs?

27
00:01:51,890 --> 00:01:55,514
DAVE: I think we have plenty of those already.

28
00:01:55,514 --> 00:01:59,138
But Dr Baker also said we have to establish

29
00:01:59,138 --> 00:02:04,320
with our interviewees whether we can identify them in our case study or

30
00:02:04,320 --> 00:02:06,790
whether they want to be anonymous.

31
00:02:06,790 --> 00:02:10,547
NATALIE: Oh, I wouldn't have thought of that.

32
00:02:10,547 --> 00:02:16,390
OK, once we've got all this information, I suppose we have to analyse it.

33
00:02:16,390 --> 00:02:18,840
DAVE: Yes, put it all together

34
00:02:18,840 --> 00:02:23,725
and choose what's relevant to the problem we're focusing on,

35
00:02:23,725 --> 00:02:26,280
and analyse that carefully to find out

36
00:02:26,280 --> 00:02:31,060
if we can identify any trends or regularities there.

37
00:02:31,060 --> 00:02:33,920
That's the main thing at this stage

38
00:02:34,080 --> 00:02:38,570
rather than concentrating on details or lots of facts.

39
00:02:38,570 --> 00:02:43,790
NATALIE: OK. And then once we've analysed that, what next?

40
00:02:43,790 --> 00:02:48,147
DAVE: Well, then we need to think about what we do

41
00:02:48,147 --> 00:02:54,088
with the data we've selected to make it as clear as possible to our readers.

42
00:02:54,088 --> 00:02:56,860
Things like graphs, or tables, or charts...

43
00:02:56,860 --> 00:02:58,410
NATALIE: Right.

44
00:02:58,410 --> 00:03:02,391
DAVE: Then the case study itself is mostly quite standard;

45
00:03:02,391 --> 00:03:06,372
we begin by presenting the problem, and giving some background,

46
00:03:06,372 --> 00:03:10,480
then go through the main sections

47
00:03:11,020 --> 00:03:14,334
but the thing that surprised me is that in a normal report we'd end

48
00:03:14,334 --> 00:03:20,200
with some suggestions to deal with the problem or need we identified

49
00:03:20,560 --> 00:03:22,296
but in a case study we end up

50
00:03:22,296 --> 00:03:27,060
with a question or a series of questions to our readers

51
00:03:27,060 --> 00:03:29,860
and they decide what ought to be done.

52
00:03:29,860 --> 00:03:33,370
NATALIE: Oh I hadn't realised that.

53
00:03:33,370 --> 00:03:41,342
NATALIE: So basically, the problem we're addressing in our case study of the Horton Castle site is why

54
00:03:41,342 --> 00:03:44,000
so few tourists are visiting it.

55
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:47,045
And we'll find out more from our interviews,

56
00:03:47,045 --> 00:03:53,135
but I did find one report on the internet that suggested that one reason might be

57
00:03:53,135 --> 00:03:56,560
because as far as transport goes, access is difficult.

58
00:03:56,560 --> 00:04:02,321
DAVE: I read that too, but that report was actually written ten years ago,

59
00:04:02,321 --> 00:04:07,259
when the road there was really bad, but that's been improved now.

60
00:04:07,259 --> 00:04:13,431
And I think there's plenty of fascinating stuff there for a really good day out,

61
00:04:13,431 --> 00:04:18,780
but you'd never realise it from the castle website - maybe that's the problem.

62
00:04:18,780 --> 00:04:22,670
NATALIE: Yes, it's really dry and boring.

63
00:04:22,670 --> 00:04:28,289
DAVE: I read somewhere a suggestion that what the castle needs is a visitor centre.

64
00:04:28,289 --> 00:04:33,533
So we could have a look for some information about that on the internet.

65
00:04:33,533 --> 00:04:35,780
What would we need to know?

66
00:04:35,780 --> 00:04:38,750
NATALIE: Well, who'd use it for a start.

67
00:04:38,750 --> 00:04:43,205
It'd be good to know what categories the visitors fell into too,

68
00:04:43,205 --> 00:04:45,980
like school parties or retired people

69
00:04:46,600 --> 00:04:50,260
but I think we'd have to talk to staff to get that information.

70
00:04:50,260 --> 00:04:54,580
DAVE: OK. And as we're thinking of suggesting a visitor centre

71
00:04:54,880 --> 00:04:57,637
we'd also have to look at potential problems.

72
00:04:57,637 --> 00:05:01,520
I mean, obviously it wouldn't be cheap to set up.

73
00:05:01,520 --> 00:05:05,243
NATALIE: No, but it could be a really good investment.

74
00:05:05,243 --> 00:05:09,960
And as it's on a historical site it'd need to get special planning permission

75
00:05:10,080 --> 00:05:12,690
I expect. That might be hard.

76
00:05:12,690 --> 00:05:17,862
DAVE: Right, especially as the only possible place for it would be at the entrance,

77
00:05:17,862 --> 00:05:20,620
and that's right in front of the castle.

78
00:05:20,620 --> 00:05:21,860
NATALIE: Mmm.

79
00:05:21,860 --> 00:05:25,360
DAVE: But it could be a good thing for the town of Horton

80
00:05:25,900 --> 00:05:29,350
At present it's a bit of a ghost town.

81
00:05:29,350 --> 00:05:33,134
Once they've left school and got any skills or qualifications,

82
00:05:33,134 --> 00:05:39,567
the young people all get out as fast as they can to get jobs in the city,

83
00:05:39,567 --> 00:05:43,730
and the only people left are children and those who've retired.

84
00:05:43,730 --> 00:05:52,362
NATALIE: Right. Something else we could investigate would be the potential damage that tourists might cause to the castle site,

85
00:05:52,362 --> 00:05:54,520
I mean their environmental impact.

86
00:05:54,520 --> 00:05:59,038
At present the tourists can just wander round wherever they want,

87
00:05:59,038 --> 00:06:02,780
but if numbers increase, there might have to be some restrictions

88
00:06:03,320 --> 00:06:05,610
like sticking to marked ways.

89
00:06:05,610 --> 00:06:10,860
And there'd need to be guides and wardens around to make sure these were enforced.

90
00:06:10,860 --> 00:06:17,310
DAVE: Yes, we could look at that too. OK, well...
